k  neighborhood he went to all black  schools  and he describes as parents who were  sharecroppers former sharecroppers from  south carolina  they had kept him relatively sheltered  from the full force  and structural violence of racism and  jim crow segregation  in the united states however all that  changes for him when he joins the army  20 years old by the time the second  world war had become a reality  no i was and i finished school in 1943  and i was 18. and that's when i became  part of the military  and you how did you become part of the  did you volunteer you  i went out and i i volunteered and i was  to go out with the next group  and when i went down to the induction  center  institutional racism smacked me right in  the face  because the sergeant was there  and he told me to go one way  when i went through the door and he told  my white friends to go another way  because my country practices promulgated  promoted institutional racism and the  military was one of the largest  to do just that and so i went into an  all-black  unit say for the officers they were  white  but all of my comrades in arms at the  time were black  induction marked the start of a brutal  new kind of education for him  beginning with two hard and disaffecting  rounds  of basic training in the jim crow south  this was first in georgia  later in mississippi followed by  maneuvers in texas louisiana  and arkansas and this extended training  was actually typical for for  african-american gis the majority of  whom were trained in combat  before they were reassigned and  retrained in  unarmed labor units bass was assigned to  the 183rd combat engineers  and this was a time when as with  civilian life  formal and informal color lines snaked  across  every aspect of military service  white soldiers lynch mobs vigilante  groups  numerous iterations of state and local  civic and law enforcement agencies they  all policed these lines as they saw fit  at the same time jim crow lawmakers at  t