d  they thanked her and they did something  i  hadn't been done in a long while they  left that room in silence  and of course she turned to me and of  course tears were coming down her face  and  she thanked me for my intervention and  and wanted to know more about my  experience at buchenwald  and i began to remember bassa's  experience was unique  but what he made of that experience and  how he and his words  began to hook things together in  subsequent decades  is instructive for us all vassa's  memories of military service and camp  liberation  and how those memories came to bear on  his interpretation of racism in the  united states it results in a call for  unity  his testimony concludes with a call for  an anti-racist coalition  building force but he also closes with a  call for vigilance  the holocaust forced a global moral  reckoning  in the united states it was a tipping  point that compelled many white  americans  to examine the state of their own  country and to get behind  african-americans demands  prevent fundamental civil and human  rights  but the realities of the holocaust also  played a significant part in forcing  white supremacists  to abandon the most explicit and  chauvinist articulations  of their world views right lest they be  associated with the unsightly  backwardness  of the recent war and it's significant  that bass re-engaged with his memories  of buchenwald around 1970.  formal narratives and institutions of  holocaust memory were on the rise in the  united states around that time  but as he so delicately puts it in his  testimony  this was a moment when many of the  promises of civil rights had begun to  falter  and the government many white americans  were clearly coming up short  in the project of reflection and  reckoning  the project of dismantling and  rebuilding their racist  foundations and bass saw that failure he  saw it  in the refusal you know of his community  to change but most especially amongst  his young students  really bass's memories open up the  possibility of  productive comparison towards solidarity  and racial justice  he asked us to consider not only what  changed after 1945 but also what  attitudes and cultures  stayed the same and in particular stayed  the same  cloaked or carried on an innuendo or in  action  [Music]  and as an educator bass concludes his  testimony um  with an important lesson for us all  and look what he did to us racism has  just about split us so many different  ways to we can't  live in harmony with one another  suspicious  all of this i discovered uh  as an adult and i didn't get it when i  was young when i should have had it  because people didn't want to deal with  it was an ugly side of our history and  ugly things we want to push under the  rug we don't want to deal with it and  i'm saying to people today  you must face it you can't laundry and  sanitize our history  you must take history with his beauty  and you must take it with its  degradation  you've got to deal with it for us to be  whole human beings and  and to make a difference and this is  especially true with young people  and so i go around telling my story and  of course i you know i get the  people saying leon what do you deal with  this this is this is not a black problem  why  i said hey it's not a black problem it's  not a white problem  it's a human problem and we've got to  face it  and as dr king says injustice anywhere  is the loss of justice everywhere you  see  words to that effect and it's true what  affects you affects  me your pain has to be my pain my pain  has to be your pain  and i must somehow convey that to the  young people  that i come to it's my commitment  and somebody says uh you you why do you  deal with that mess you know i  i said i'm sorry i know it's been 40  something years  but that doesn't make it go away it only  makes us  become more aware that we today have to  do something that  to stop that which created  the final solution and that something  is racism really racism is at the root  of all of this  under that umbrella comes bigotry and  prejudice and discrimination  unemployment  people who are unemployable large  institutions filled with those who are  drug addicts and those who are  criminals all because somehow we have  come to grips with that institution  called racism and we have to because we  see the ultimate of racism which was  what i saw at buchenwall  and so what do we do do we as educators  and  and i use it in the broadest sense  because we're all teachers of the young  in our way of life do we convey to them  the best that we can be or do we give  them  the worst side that we have do we  promote  racism through our apathy  when we hear and see things we say  nothing  because we don't want to jeopardize that  which is important to us our investment  in a job  our investment among friends we don't  want to disturb our families so  no matter what people say or do just  leave it alone sweep it under the rug  and somehow it'll go away but the  skinheads are with us  the clan is with us it may be dressed in  brooks brothers suit with attached cases  they may be in doctors they may be  lawyers they may be  the guy who drives the bus but they're  still with us

